  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "For the curve $y=\\frac{3}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}+2,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{3}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{0}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{3}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}+2$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{11}{4}).$",
